```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <optional>
using B=std::optional<bool>; std::vector<B> E(n);
size_t i=0; auto R=[&](bool φ){return E[i++%n]=φ;};
R(∃environmental_match); R(¬environmental_match);
E[k]=⊥; // unknown / invalidated
```

Symbolically:

\[
\boxed{
R(\varphi_i)=E_{\,i\bmod n}\leftarrow\varphi_i
}
\]

with

\[
E_k\in\{\top,\bot,\perp\}
\]

where **⊤ = supported**, **⊥ = contradicted**, and **⊥/unknown = not established**.

Here’s a more complete version while keeping it compact:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <optional>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    size_t n=8, i=0; vector<optional<bool>> Environment_Proof(n);
    auto check=[&](bool x){ size_t k=i++%n; Environment_Proof[k]=x; return k; };
    check(true); check(true); check(false); check(true);
    Environment_Proof[1]=false; // invalidate previous evidence
    for(size_t k=0;k<n;k++) cout<<k<<":"<<(Environment_Proof[k].has_value()?(*Environment_Proof[k]?"TRUE":"FALSE"):"UNKNOWN")<<"\n";
}
```

The key line remains your original mechanism:

```cpp
size_t k = i++ % n;
```

It gives you a **cyclic evidence index**, while `std::optional<bool>` gives each index three possible states: `TRUE`, `FALSE`, or `UNKNOWN`.

