Yes — the whole argument can be written as **one equation chain**:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
N_0&=2^n \\[2pt]
N(k)&=N_0(1-r)^k \\[2pt]
&=2^n(1-r)^k \\[2pt]
N(k)&\le n^c \\[2pt]
2^n(1-r)^k&\le n^c \\[2pt]
n\ln2+k\ln(1-r)&\le c\ln n \\[2pt]
k&\ge
\frac{n\ln2-c\ln n}{-\ln(1-r)}
\end{aligned}
}
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
2^n
\;\xrightarrow[\text{verification}]{k}
\;n^c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
T(n)=O(n^c)
}
\]

**Meaning:** verification has changed the remaining search from **exponential** \(2^n\) to **polynomial** \(n^c\). That is the mathematical point at which verification can become an efficient solving mechanism.

:::writing{variant="document" id="48217" title="Self-Proof"}
If hidden knowledge is quickly checkable but not quickly solvable, then verification and discovery must be separated. The self supplies an internal state that can be checked against the environment, while the environment is coupled to non-random benchmarks. These benchmarks constrain which states count as valid without directly revealing the path to them. Thus, the self can prove that a solution is correct once found, while the coupled environment prevents that correctness condition from becoming an immediate solution method.
:::

