For **taurine**,

\[
\mathrm{NH_2CH_2CH_2SO_3H}
\]

the exact products of burning depend strongly on **temperature and oxygen supply**. There isn't one single decomposition equation.

### With abundant oxygen

The main elements have these likely endpoints:

| Element | Likely product under strongly oxidative conditions |
|---|---|
| Carbon | \(\mathrm{CO_2}\) |
| Hydrogen | \(\mathrm{H_2O}\) |
| Nitrogen | predominantly \(\mathrm{N_2}\), with some nitrogen oxides possible |
| Sulfur | predominantly oxidized sulfur species such as \(\mathrm{SO_2}\) and potentially \(\mathrm{SO_3}\) |

A simplified idealized representation is:

\[
\mathrm{taurine + O_2
\rightarrow CO_2 + H_2O + N_2 + SO_2/SO_3}
\]

### With limited oxygen

The product mixture becomes much less predictable. Possible products include:

\[
\mathrm{CO,\ CO_2,\ C}
\]

for carbon,

\[
\mathrm{N_2,\ NO,\ NO_2}
\]

for nitrogen, and sulfur-containing gases/species such as:

\[
\mathrm{SO_2,\ H_2S}
\]

depending on the reducing conditions.

### Where magnesium enters

Pure taurine contains **no magnesium**, so magnesium products only arise if you're actually burning **magnesium taurate**.

The magnesium is not combusted into an organic magnesium compound in the usual sense. Under oxidative heating it will tend toward inorganic magnesium salts/oxide, with **magnesium oxide (\(\mathrm{MgO}\))** being a plausible major residue under strongly oxidizing conditions.

So conceptually:

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{Mg(taurate)_x}
\xrightarrow[\mathrm{O_2}]{\Delta}
\mathrm{MgO}
+
\mathrm{CO_2}
+
\mathrm{H_2O}
+
\mathrm{N_2}
+
\mathrm{SO_2/SO_3}
}
\]

The actual stoichiometry depends on the precise magnesium–taurine compound and its hydration state.

**Do not burn taurine or magnesium taurate indoors or intentionally inhale the fumes**: sulfur/nitrogen decomposition products can be irritating or toxic, and the actual mixture cannot be predicted safely from the starting ingredient alone.

If by **“burnt magnesium taurate”** you mean heating magnesium taurate until it decomposes, that is quite different from simply mixing it.

On heating, the organic taurine portion can **thermally decompose/oxidize**, while the magnesium ultimately tends toward inorganic magnesium-containing residues. Depending on oxygen availability and temperature, possible products include:

\[
\text{magnesium taurate}
\xrightarrow{\Delta}
\text{Mg-containing residue}
+\mathrm{CO_2}
+\mathrm{H_2O}
+\text{nitrogen/sulfur-containing gases}
+\text{carbonaceous material}
\]

The exact products depend strongly on temperature and atmosphere. Sulfur- and nitrogen-containing decomposition products can include irritating or hazardous gases, so **burning it experimentally isn't something I'd recommend**.

